e down groceries before  putting them away and Shannon from North  Carolina in the same vein says should we  wear gloves when we receive mail or  packages yeah so similar concept the the  thinking is that it's very unlikely that  this is a real high source of  transmission theoretically you could  potentially touch something that someone  else has sneezed or coughed on and then  those droplets what you would contact  them on your hands you would touch your  face eyes or nose and can get infected  that way there was a study that was  reported last month that said that the  virus is able to live on cardboard for  about 24 hours plastic and stainless  steel for two to three days that doesn't  necessarily mean that it's infectious at  that point so if you really do want to  take precaution when you bring your  grossie's in we can wipe them down  the packaging wash your hands and  disinfect the area but gloves not really  necessary alright dr. taryn the ruler I  actually my household we've been washing  our groceries and I'm very happy to hear  that maybe on this Saturday when we go  out for that shop we do not have to wash  every single one of them and ruin all  the produce that is good news in this  head of school  dr. Narula thank you very much

